      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining the Metric Provider**\n\nI've decided to use Google Cloud Monitoring as the data provider and will ensure its integration is clearly defined within the Python script, `query_metric_window.py`. I'm focusing on making the provider selection explicit in the code.\n\n\n**Implementing the Query Script**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in the coding phase, building the `query_metric_window.py` script. The Google Cloud Monitoring client is instantiated, and I've integrated the necessary placeholders for user input: project ID, metric type, and the time window. I'm focusing on ensuring the script is functional and that it makes clear to the user the project ID is required, the metric type is required, and the time window start and end are required.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789861450727,
          "start": 1789861447000
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc0e4558001FbVnnxZfvxIZN1",
      "message_id": "msg_0bc0e3fba00171tRz6WD6AVAsH",
      "session_id": "ses_f43f1c0ebffejMu5kTuS11bw5H",
      "time_created": 1789861447000,
      "time_updated": 1789861450727
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "callID": "1qbboIBxvBdg8ZtR",
        "metadata": {
          "google": {
            "thoughtSignature": ""
          }
        },
        "state": {
          "input": {
            "content": "from google.cloud import monitoring_v3\nfrom google.protobuf import timestamp_pb2\nimport datetime\n\ndef query_metric_window(project_id: str, metric_type: str, end_time: datetime.datetime, minutes: int = 60):\n    \"\"\"Queries recent datapoints for a named metric over a time window using Google Cloud Monitoring.\n\n    Args:\n        project_id: Your Google Cloud project ID.\n        metric_type: The type of the metric to query (e.g., \"compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/usage_time\").\n        end_time: The end time for the query window (datetime object).\n        minutes: The duration of the time window in minutes, ending at `end_time`. Defaults to 60 minutes.\n    \"\"\"\n    client = monitoring_v3.MetricServiceClient()\n    project_name = f\"projects/{project_id}\"\n\n    start_time = end_time - datetime.timedelta(minutes=minutes)\n\n    # Convert datetime objects to protobuf Timestamp objects\n    start_timestamp = timestamp_pb2.Timestamp()\n    start_timestamp.FromDatetime(start_time)\n    end_timestamp = timestamp_pb2.Timestamp()\n    end_timestamp.FromDatetime(end_time)\n\n    interval = monitoring_v3.TimeInterval(\n        end_time=end_timestamp,\n        start_time=start_timestamp\n    )\n\n    query_filter = f'metric.type = \"{metric_type}\"'\n\n    response = client.list_time_series(\n        request={\n            \"name\": project_name,\n            \"filter\": query_filter,\n            \"interval\": interval,\n            \"view\": monitoring_v3.ListTimeSeriesRequest.TimeSeriesView.FULL,\n        }\n    )\n\n    print(f\"Metric: {metric_type}\")\n    for series in response.time_series:\n        print(f\"  Resource: {series.resource.type}, Labels: {series.resource.labels}\")\n        for point in series.points:\n            point_time = point.interval.end_time.ToDatetime()\n            value = point.value.double_value if point.value.HasField(\"double_value\") else point.value.int64_value\n            print(f\"    Time: {point_time}, Value: {value}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example usage: Replace with your actual project ID and metric type\n    PROJECT_ID = \"your-google-cloud-project-id\"\n    METRIC_TYPE = \"compute.googleapis.com/instance/cpu/usage_time\" # Example metric\n    \n    # Query for the last 60 minutes\n    query_metric_window(PROJECT_ID, METRIC_TYPE, datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc), minutes=60)\n",
            "filePath": "query_metric_window.py"
          },
